"Internal Error ()\nciteturn7view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.rs/comemo/0.4.0/comemo/attr.memoize.html\",\"lineno\":null}); \nURL https://docs.rs/comemo/0.4.0/comemo/attr.memoize.html is not accessible via this tool.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing the File System Events API (https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Darwin/Conceptual/FSEvents_ProgGuide/UsingtheFSEventsFramework/UsingtheFSEventsFramework.html)\nciteturn7view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Darwin/Conceptual/FSEvents_ProgGuide/UsingtheFSEventsFramework/UsingtheFSEventsFramework.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 356\nL0: 【0†Documentation Archive】 【1†Developer】\nL1: \nL2: Search\nL3: \nL4: Search Documentation Archive [Input]\nL5: \nL6: # File System Events Programming Guide\nL7: \nL8: PDF Companion File\nL9: \nL10:   * Table of Contents\nL11:   * [Button: Download Sample Code]\nL12: \nL13: 【2†Next】【3†Previous】\nL14: # Using the File System Events API\nL15: \nL16: The File System Events API consists of several distinct groups of functions. You can obtain general information about volumes and events by using functions that begin with `FSEvents`. You can create a new event stream, perform operations on the stream, and so on using functions that begin with `FSEventStream`.\nL17: \nL18: The life cycle of a file system events stream is as follows:\nL19:   1. The application creates a stream by calling `【4†FSEventStreamCreate】` or `【5†FSEventStreamCreateRelativeToDevice】`.\nL20: \nL21:   2. The application schedules the stream on the run loop by calling `【6†FSEventStreamScheduleWithRunLoop】`.\nL22: \nL23:   3. The application tells the file system events daemon to start sending events by calling `【7†FSEventStreamStart】`.\nL24: \nL25:   4. The application services events as they arrive. The API posts events by calling the callback function specified in step 1.\nL26:   5. The application tells the daemon to stop sending events by calling `【8†FSEventStreamStop】`.\nL27: \nL28:   6. If the application needs to restart the stream, go to step 3.\nL29: \nL30:   7. The application unschedules the event from its run loop by calling `【9†FSEventStreamUnscheduleFromRunLoop】`.\nL31: \nL32:   8. The application invalidates the stream by calling `【10†FSEventStreamInvalidate】`.\nL33: \nL34:   9. The application releases its reference to the stream by calling `【11†FSEventStreamRelease】`.\nL35: These steps are explained in more detail in the sections that follow.\nL36: ## Adding Include Directives\nL37: \nL38: Before you use the file system event stream API, you must include the Core Services framework as follows:\nL39: \nL40:     #include <CoreServices/CoreServices.h>\nL41: \nL42: When you compile, you must include the Core Services Framework by adding it to your target in Xcode or by adding the flag `-framework CoreServices` to your linker flags on the command line or in a Makefile.\nL43: ## Creating an Event Stream\nL44: \nL45: The file system events API supports two types of event streams: per-disk event streams and a per-host event streams. Before you can create a stream, you must decide which type of stream to create: a per-host event stream or a per-disk event stream. You can create these streams by calling the functions `【4†FSEventStreamCreate】` and `【5†FSEventStreamCreateRelativeToDevice】`, respectively.\nL46: A per-host event stream consists of events whose IDs are increasing with respect to other events on that host. These IDs are guaranteed to be unique with one exception: if additional disks are added from another computer that was also running OS X v10.5 or later, historical IDs may conflict between these volumes. Any new events will automatically start after the highest-numbered historical ID for any attached drive.\nL47: A per-disk event stream, by contrast, consists of events whose IDs are increasing with respect to previous events on that disk. It does not have any relationship with other events on other disks, and thus you must create a separate event stream for each physical device that you wish to monitor.\nL48: In general, if you are writing software that requires persistence, you should use per-disk streams to avoid any confusion due to ID conflicts. By contrast, per-host streams are most convenient if you are monitoring for changes in a directory or tree of directories during normal execution, such as watching a queue directory.\nL49: Note: Because disks can be modified by computers running earlier versions of OS X (or potentially other operating systems), you should treat the events list as advisory rather than a definitive list of all changes to the volume. If a disk is modified by a computer running a previous version of OS X, the historical log is discarded.\nL50: \nL51: For example, backup software should still periodically perform a full sweep of any volume to ensure that no changes fall through the cracks.\nL52: If you are monitoring files on the root file system, either stream mechanism will behave similarly.\nL53: For example, the following snippet shows how to create an event stream:\nL54: \nL55:         /* Define variables and create a CFArray object containing\nL56: \nL57:            CFString objects containing paths to watch.\nL58: \nL59:          */\nL60: \nL61:         CFStringRef mypath = CFSTR(\"/path/to/scan\");\nL62: \nL63:         CFArrayRef pathsToWatch = CFArrayCreate(NULL, (const void **)&mypath, 1, NULL);\nL64: \nL65:         void *callbackInfo = NULL; // could put stream-specific data here.\nL66: \nL67:         FSEventStreamRef stream;\nL68:         CFAbsoluteTime latency = 3.0; /* Latency in seconds */\nL69: \nL70: \nL71: \nL72:         /* Create the stream, passing in a callback */\nL73: \nL74:         stream = FSEventStreamCreate(NULL,\nL75: \nL76:             &myCallbackFunction,\nL77: \nL78:             callbackInfo,\nL79: \nL80:             pathsToWatch,\nL81: \nL82:             kFSEventStreamEventIdSinceNow, /* Or a previous event ID */\nL83: \nL84:             latency,\nL85: \nL86:             kFSEventStreamCreateFlagNone /* Flags explained in reference */\nL87: \nL88:         );\nL89: Once you have created an event stream, you must schedule it on your application’s run loop. To do this, call `FSEventStreamScheduleWithRunLoop`, passing in the newly-created stream, a reference to your run loop, and a run loop mode. For more information about run loops, read 【12†Run Loops】.\nL90: If you don’t already have a run loop, you will need to devote a thread to this task. After creating a thread using your API of choice, call `【13†CFRunLoopGetCurrent】` to allocate an initial run loop for that thread. Any future calls to `【13†CFRunLoopGetCurrent】` will return the same run loop.\nL91: For example, the following snippet shows how to schedule a stream, called `stream`, on the current thread’s run loop (not yet running):\nL92: \nL93:         FSEventStreamRef stream;\nL94: \nL95:         /* Create the stream before calling this. */\nL96: \nL97:         FSEventStreamScheduleWithRunLoop(stream, CFRunLoopGetCurrent(),         kCFRunLoopDefaultMode);\nL98: The final step in setting up an event stream is to call `FSEventStreamStart`. This function tells the event stream to begin sending events. Its sole parameter is the event stream to start.\nL99: \nL100: Once the event stream has been created and scheduled, if your run loop is not already running, you should start it by calling `【14†CFRunLoopRun】`.\nL101: ## Handling Events\nL102: \nL103: Your event handler callback must conform to the prototype for `【15†FSEventStreamCallback】`. The parameters are described in the reference documentation for the `FSEventStreamCallback` data type.\nL104: Your event handler receives three lists: a list of paths, a list of identifiers, and a list of flags. In effect, these represent a list of events. The first event consists of the first entry taken from each of the arrays, and so on. Your handler must iterate through these lists, processing the events as needed.\nL105: For each event, you should scan the directory at the specified path, processing its contents as desired. Normally, you need to scan only the exact directory specified by the path. However, there are three situations in which this is not the case:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReadDirectoryChangesW function (winbase.h) - Win32 apps | Microsoft Learn (https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw)\nciteturn7view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-readdirectorychangesw; Total lines: 208\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】 【1†Skip to Ask Learn chat experience】\nL1: \nL2: This browser is no longer supported.\nL3: \nL4: Upgrade to Microsoft Edge to take advantage of the latest features, security updates, and technical support.\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Download Microsoft Edge†go.microsoft.com】 【3†More info about Internet Explorer and Microsoft Edge】\nL7: \nL8: Table of contents Exit editor mode\nL9: \nL10: Ask Learn Ask Learn\nL11: \nL12: Reading mode Table of contents 【1†Read in English】 Add Add to Plans 【4†Edit†github.com】\nL13: \nL14: * * *\nL15: \nL16: Copy Markdown Print\nL17: \nL18: * * *\nL19: \nL20: Note\nL21: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try 【1†signing in】 or changing directories.\nL22: \nL23: Access to this page requires authorization. You can try changing directories.\nL24: # ReadDirectoryChangesW function (winbase.h)\nL25: \nL26: Feedback\nL27: \nL28: Summarize this article for me\nL29: \nL30: ## In this article\nL31: \nL32: Retrieves information that describes the changes within the specified directory. The function does not report changes to the specified directory itself.\nL33: \nL34: To track changes on a volume, see 【5†change journals】.\nL35: ## Syntax\nL36: \nL37:     `BOOL ReadDirectoryChangesW(\nL38:       [in]                HANDLE                          hDirectory,\nL39:       [out]               LPVOID                          lpBuffer,\nL40:       [in]                DWORD                           nBufferLength,\nL41:       [in]                BOOL                            bWatchSubtree,\nL42:       [in]                DWORD                           dwNotifyFilter,\nL43:       [out, optional]     LPDWORD                         lpBytesReturned,\nL44:       [in, out, optional] LPOVERLAPPED                    lpOverlapped,\nL45:       [in, optional]      LPOVERLAPPED_COMPLETION_ROUTINE lpCompletionRoutine\nL46:     );\nL47:     `\nL48: ## Parameters\nL49: \nL50: `[in] hDirectory`\nL51: \nL52: A handle to the directory to be monitored. This directory must be opened with the FILE_LIST_DIRECTORY access right, or an access right such as GENERIC_READ that includes the FILE_LIST_DIRECTORY access right.\nL53: \nL54: `[out] lpBuffer`\nL55: A pointer to the DWORD-aligned formatted buffer in which the read results are to be returned. The structure of this buffer is defined by the 【6†FILE_NOTIFY_INFORMATION】 structure. This buffer is filled either synchronously or asynchronously, depending on how the directory is opened and what value is given to the lpOverlapped parameter. For more information, see the Remarks section.\nL56: \nL57: `[in] nBufferLength`\nL58: \nL59: The size of the buffer that is pointed to by the lpBuffer parameter, in bytes.\nL60: \nL61: `[in] bWatchSubtree`\nL62: If this parameter is TRUE, the function monitors the directory tree rooted at the specified directory. If this parameter is FALSE, the function monitors only the directory specified by the hDirectory parameter.\nL63: \nL64: `[in] dwNotifyFilter`\nL65: \nL66: The filter criteria that the function checks to determine if the wait operation has completed. This parameter can be one or more of the following values.\nL67: \nL68: Value  | Meaning\nL69: --- | ---\nL70: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_FILE_NAME\nL71: 0x00000001\nL72:  | Any file name change in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return. Changes include renaming, creating, or deleting a file.\nL73: \nL74: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_DIR_NAME\nL75: 0x00000002\nL76:  | Any directory-name change in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return. Changes include creating or deleting a directory.\nL77: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_ATTRIBUTES\nL78: 0x00000004\nL79:  | Any attribute change in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return.\nL80: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_SIZE\nL81: 0x00000008\nL82:  | Any file-size change in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return. The operating system detects a change in file size only when the file is written to the disk. For operating systems that use extensive caching, detection occurs only when the cache is sufficiently flushed.\nL83: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_LAST_WRITE\nL84: 0x00000010\nL85:  | Any change to the last write-time of files in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return. The operating system detects a change to the last write-time only when the file is written to the disk. For operating systems that use extensive caching, detection occurs only when the cache is sufficiently flushed.\nL86: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_LAST_ACCESS\nL87: 0x00000020\nL88:  | Any change to the last access time of files in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return.\nL89: \nL90: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_CREATION\nL91: 0x00000040\nL92:  | Any change to the creation time of files in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return.\nL93: FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_SECURITY\nL94: 0x00000100\nL95:  | Any security-descriptor change in the watched directory or subtree causes a change notification wait operation to return.\nL96: \nL97: `[out, optional] lpBytesReturned`\nL98: \nL99: For synchronous calls, this parameter receives the number of bytes transferred into the lpBuffer parameter. For asynchronous calls, this parameter is undefined. You must use an asynchronous notification technique to retrieve the number of bytes transferred.\nL100: \nL101: `[in, out, optional] lpOverlapped`\nL102: A pointer to an 【7†OVERLAPPED】 structure that supplies data to be used during asynchronous operation. Otherwise, this value is NULL. The Offset and OffsetHigh members of this structure are not used.\nL103: \nL104: `[in, optional] lpCompletionRoutine`\nL105: \nL106: A pointer to a completion routine to be called when the operation has been completed or canceled and the calling thread is in an alertable wait state. For more information about this completion routine, see 【8†FileIOCompletionRoutine】.\nL107: ## Return value\nL108: \nL109: If the function succeeds, the return value is nonzero. For synchronous calls, this means that the operation succeeded. For asynchronous calls, this indicates that the operation was successfully queued.\nL110: \nL111: If the function fails, the return value is zero. To get extended error information, call 【9†GetLastError】.\nL112: \nL113: If the network redirector or the target file system does not support this operation, the function fails with ERROR_INVALID_FUNCTION.\nL114: ## Remarks\nL115: \nL116: To obtain a handle to a directory, use the 【10†CreateFile】 function with the FILE_FLAG_BACKUP_SEMANTICS flag.\nL117: \nL118: A call to ReadDirectoryChangesW can be completed synchronously or asynchronously. To specify asynchronous completion, open the directory with 【10†CreateFile】 as shown above, but additionally specify the FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED attribute in the dwFlagsAndAttributes parameter. Then specify an 【7†OVERLAPPED】 structure when you call ReadDirectoryChangesW.\nL119: When you first call ReadDirectoryChangesW, the system allocates a buffer to store change information. This buffer is associated with the directory handle until it is closed and its size does not change during its lifetime. Directory changes that occur between calls to this function are added to the buffer and then returned with the next call.\nL120: If the buffer overflows, ReadDirectoryChangesW will still return true, but the entire contents of the buffer are discarded and the lpBytesReturned parameter will be zero, which indicates that your buffer was too small to hold all of the changes that occurred.\n"
